!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/fixtures/validate-binary-identity.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  The three rules that compare two decoded values, over `bytes` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §7.5's duplicate rule and
  §5.2's FIXED check, [TSON-DATA] §2.6's key identity). Each delegates the comparison to the type's own
  equality contract, and a host language whose binary type carries reference equality satisfies none of
  them while looking like it does: the schema loads, the document reads, and every comparison silently
  answers "different".

  Every vector over this fixture states one value **in one spelling, twice**, so none of them depends on
  what §7.5's contract says about two spellings of one octet string — that question is open (see the
  register) and a vector turning on it would fail a processor that answers it the other way. A value equals
  itself under any contract there could be, which is what makes these safe and what makes them sharp: an
  implementation failing them has no equality at all rather than the wrong one.
  """
{
  digests => set<bytes>
  by_hash => { bytes => text }

  receipt => {
    seen:  digests
    named: by_hash
    tag:   bytes = "SGk="
  }
}
